+    => factorDivisor((N -Int pyMod(N, D)) /Int D, D)
+    requires N >Int 1 andBool D >Int 0
+         andBool pyMod(N, D) ==Int 0
+  rule factorDivisor(N:Int, D:Int)
+    => factorDivisor(N, D +Int 1)
+    requires N >Int 1 andBool D >Int 0
+         andBool pyMod(N, D) =/=Int 0
+endmodule

codex
The main proof remains CPU-active at roughly 1.3 GB and has no failure state. This is the same expensive recursive-oracle simplification seen in the invariant proof, so I’m allowing it to complete rather than weakening the theorem.
